{"name":"Hygge Green","hex":"#e0e1d8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hygge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of cosiness, comfort, contentment and conviviality. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cosy, convivial."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hygge"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hygge","blocks":[{"text":"Hygge is a word in Danish and Norwegian that describes a cozy, contented mood evoked by comfort and conviviality. As a cultural category with its sets of associated practices, hygge has more or less the same meaning in both places and in both languages; however, the emphasis on hygge as a core part of Danish culture is a recent phenomenon, dating to the late 20th century. In the 21st century, the concept has also been familiarized abroad."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/A_Hygge_moment.jpg/330px-A_Hygge_moment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygge","wikidata":"Q311045"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg/500px-20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg/1280px-20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg/500px-20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_%2851453642905%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Hygge\" sign in a restaurant in Nørrebro","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20210904_Hygge_DSF5493_(51453642905).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pend